---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Learn more about the purpose and design principles behind SEED."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">About SEED</span>
        <h1>A resource centre designed to feel calm, useful, and credible.</h1>
        <p>
          SEED starts with teachers. The aim is to share practical resources in a way that feels
          professional and uncluttered, while keeping the technical setup easy to maintain.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Principles</span>
        <h3>What guides this first version</h3>
        <ul class="list-clean">
          <li>Use static pages for speed, stability, and simple hosting</li>
          <li>Keep content in structured files instead of hardcoding it into layouts</li>
          <li>Design with warmth and whitespace rather than visual noise</li>
          <li>Make future expansion possible without a rebuild from scratch</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <div class="quote-card stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Audience</span>
        <h2>Teachers first, with a path for wider support later.</h2>
        <p>
          The initial content and language are teacher-focused, but the structure leaves room for
          future sections for parents, carers, and students when needed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Maintenance</span>
        <h3>Built for beginner-friendly upkeep</h3>
        <p>
          To add a new resource, edit one JSON file. To publish a new post, add one more object to
          the blog data file. That is enough for a small site to stay manageable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
